.Dd February 1, 2026
.Dt VFS 9
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm vfs_mount ,
.Nm vfs_lookup ,
.Nm vfs_open ,
.Nm vfs_register_filesystem
.Nd virtual file system interface
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In vfs/vfs.h
.Ft int
.Fn vfs_mount "const char *device" "const char *path" "const char *type" "uint32_t flags" "void *data"
.Ft "fs_node_t *"
.Fn vfs_lookup "fs_node_t *root" "const char *path"
.Ft "void"
.Fn vfs_register_filesystem "filesystem_t *fs"
.Ft "size_t"
.Fn read_fs "fs_node_t *node" "off_t offset" "size_t size" "uint8_t *buffer"
.Ft "size_t"
.Fn write_fs "fs_node_t *node" "off_t offset" "size_t size" "const uint8_t *buffer"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The Substrate Virtual File System (VFS) provides a unified interface for accessing different filesystems.
.SS Mounting
.Fn vfs_mount
mounts a filesystem of
.Fa type
on the directory specified by
.Fa path .
The
.Fa device
argument specifies the source device (e.g. "/dev/hda"), and
.Fa flags
and
.Fa data
provide filesystem-specific options.
.SS Lookup
.Fn vfs_lookup
resolves a pathname to a
.Vt fs_node_t
structure, starting from the given
.Fa root .
This handles directory traversal and mount points transparently.
.SS Registration
.Fn vfs_register_filesystem
registers a new filesystem driver with the kernel. The
.Vt filesystem_t
structure must provide a
.Fn mount
callback.
.SS I/O Operations
.Fn read_fs
and
.Fn write_fs
are wrappers around the node-specific read/write method pointers. They perform validity checks and invoke the underlying driver.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn vfs_mount
returns 0 on success, or -1 on error.
.Fn vfs_lookup
returns a pointer to the found node, or NULL if not found.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pmap 9 ,
.Xr vfs_mkdir 9
.Sh HISTORY
The VFS subsystem first appeared in Substrate.
